<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  What a hardened-runtime build of REC Exam Browser asks macOS for, and
  what it conspicuously does not (Phase 14f).

  Present: the two JIT entitlements Chromium needs to run at all, and
  nothing else.

  Absent, deliberately, and each one is a line in docs/PROCTORING.md §5:
    com.apple.security.device.camera
    com.apple.security.device.audio-input
    com.apple.security.device.microphone
    com.apple.security.personal-information.*
    com.apple.security.files.user-selected.*

  An entitlements file is the most legible statement a macOS application
  makes about what it is capable of, so this one is kept short on purpose: a
  reviewer at a college should be able to read it in full in ten seconds and
  see that the camera is not in it.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>
</plist>
